Traditional hydraulic workgroups have been completely replaced by electric drive systems consisting of electric cylinders and electric drive motors, which means virtually no fluid.The Lynx T7X uses only one quart of environmentally friendly coolant, compared to 57 gallons in its diesel/hydraulic equivalent model.
The T7X operates with zero emissions and reduces noise and vibration from the machine.It works quietly and efficiently in noise-sensitive areas and indoors, significantly reducing sound levels and improving the working environment.It can also significantly reduce day-to-day operating costs, given the reduction in annual maintenance costs and the elimination of diesel, engine oil, diesel exhaust fluid and hydraulic components.
While the new T7X is eco-designed, it’s also a smart machine, with the software that Bobcat devices are known for communicating via two-way telematics.The platform provides complete data on machine performance, as well as operator-centric data to change machine preferences, adjust performance to specific job situations, and upgrade product features.This includes variable drive speeds at full torque and other features not possible with diesel hydraulics.
“Like Doosan Lynx, we believe electrification, automation and connectivity go hand in hand,” said John Scannell, Moog Chairman and CEO.“We are proud to partner with Doosan Bobcat to bring our comprehensive electrification capabilities, including hardware and intelligent software, to the world’s first all-electric loader. The T7X is an exciting step forward for the construction industry.”
