●Don’t truck or unload wood flooring in the rain, snow or other humid conditions.
●lt is essential that basements and crawl spaces are dry.Crawl spaces must be a minimum of 18″ from the ground to underside of joists. A vapor barrier must be established in crawl spaces using 6mil black polyethylene film with joints overlapped and taped.
●Hardwood flooring must acclimate for as long as necessary to meet minimum installation requirements for moisture content.Always use a moisture meter to monitor the flooring and job-site conditions as they acclimate, until the wood is neither gaining nor losing moisture.
●Wood sub-floors must be dry and free of wax, paint, oil, and debris.Replace any water-damaged or delaminated sub-flooring or underlayments.
●Concrete slabs must be of high compressive strength with minimum 3,000 psi. In addition, concrete sub-floors must be dry, smooth and free of wax, paint,oil, grease, dirt, non-compatible sealers and drywall compound etc.
●Lightweight concrete that has a dry density of 100 pounds or less percubic foot is not suitable for engineered wood floors.To check for lightweight concrete, draw a nail cross the top. lf it leaves an indentation, it is probably lightweight concrete.
If it reads 4.5% or above, then this slab must be checked using calcium chloride tests. Flooring should not be laid if the test result exceeds 3 lbs per 1000 sqft of vapor emissionin a 24-hour period.
●Stagger the ends of boards and maintain at least 6″ between end joints on all adjacent rows.
●Start installation parallel to the longest unbroken wall. An out silde wall is often the best.
●Apply urethane adhesive using a trowel recommended by your glue manufacturer. Do not use a water-based adhesive with this hardwood flooring product.
●Install a starter board along the edge of the working line and begin installation. Boards should be installed left to right with the tongue side of the board facing the staring wall.
●A vapor retarder of asphalt -saturated paper can be installed on the sub-floor before instaling hardwood floor. This will prevent moisture from below and may prevent squeaks.
●Lay one row of boards along the entire length of the working line, with the tongue facing away from the wall.
●Blind nail at 45°angle through the tongue 1″-3″ from the end joints and every 4-6″ in between along the length of the starter boards.Denser species may require pre-drilling the holes in the tongue. It might be necessary to blind nail the first few rows.
●Thoroughly clean, sweep, and vacuum installed floor and inspect the floor for scratches, gaps and other imperfections. The new floor can be used after 12-24 hours.
●Sub-floor flatness is critical to the success of a floating floor installation. A flatness tolerance of 1/8″ in a 10-foot radius is required for floating floor installation.
●Clean excess glue from between boards with a clean cotton cloth.Tape each board together at side and end seams using 3-M Blue Tape. Allow glue to set before continuing installation of subsequent rows.
●Thoroughly clean, sweep, and vacuum installed floor and inspect the floor for scratches, gaps and other imperfections. The new floor can be used after 12 24 hours.
