Nitrates (NO3) in the aquarium are the last in the chain of chemical compounds in the nitrogen cycle. Read about the two previous, much more dangerous and hidden killers in the aquarium in my posts, at the links below.
Different types of fish have different sensitivity to nitrates. The uppermost limit of the safe tolerance is considered to be 40-50 mg / l. Although with a gradual increase, fish can even transfer 400 mg / l. But all experts agree that nitrates should be kept at the lowest possible level.
But in the case of nitrates there is one nuance. Tap water already contains some amount nitrates, and if there are many of them, then this method may not work.
Now, in modern aquarium filters, fillers are already provided for all occasions, and in the case of high nitrates, including. These are either specially soaked sponges, for example, Juwel Nitrax.
Almost every manufacturer of aquarium products has a special additive in stock that can instantly lower the level of nitrates in the water. For instance, Tetra Nitrateminus, or Sera Toxivec...
