Ammonia in the water. Where from?
But under normal conditions, in an aquarium with a well-established biological balance, and in which, in sufficient quantities there are usefulnitrifyingbacteria (Nitrosomonas) - the concentration of ammonia is close to zero and does not pose any danger to the inhabitants of the aquarium. The fact is that these bacteria process ammonia into still dangerous nitrites, and already nitrites are processed by other bacteria into relatively safe nitrates.
