A rectal Stool management system (SMS) is a thin plastic tube that is inserted into the rectum to collect stool (poop).
The SMS is used to collect stool and control diarrhea for patients in the hospital who cannot get out of bed to use the toilet.
There’s a small risk that the SMS could cause an ulcer in the rectum which may be painful or cause bleeding.
The SMS may effectively divert stool into the bag which could protect a patient’s wounds from contamination and reduce the risk of infection or of the patient’s skin becoming ulcerated.
Collecting stool in an SMS may reduce odor from diarrhea and preserve a patient’s dignity.
Some patients may find the SMS uncomfortable or embarrassing.
